From: Leo Audio OS <leo-audio-os@localhost>
Date: 2026-08-29
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] leo: add HiFi volume state and read-back

Implements the independent HiFi volume on the ES9018 "Volume" kcontrol.

Kernel-derived facts, read out of the MoKee boot kernel image
(snd_kcontrol_new -> soc_mixer_control + TLV, base 0xffffffc000080000):

    min = 0, max = 255, reg = 15, rreg = 16, invert = 1
    TLV = DB_SCALE(min = -127.50 dB, step = 0.50 dB)
    => dB = -127.50 + 0.50 * ctl_value; higher control value == louder
    205 = -25.0 dB (mixer_paths.xml default on both MIUI and MoKee)
    213 = -21.0 dB   225 = -15.0 dB   229 = -13.0 dB   253 = -1.0 dB

Rules enforced here:
  * hard clamp to [LEO_HIFI_CTL_FLOOR(205), LEO_HIFI_CTL_CEIL(237)];
  * every write is read back; a mismatch restores 205 and reports
    LEO_FAIL_READBACK_SOFT;
  * out-of-range requests are rejected without changing the value
    (LEO_FAIL_VOL_RANGE);
  * large changes are applied in bounded frames on top of the codec's own
    "Volume Ramp Rate";
  * a failure never blocks boot and never fails set_parameters().

dB logging goes through leo_hifi_ctl_to_db(), which carries the sign
separately.  Deriving it from the quotient would print "0.5 dB" for
control value 254 (真值 -0.5 dB) because C integer division truncates
toward zero -- a log-only defect, but one that would corrupt evidence.

Deliberately NOT changed: platform_init() still does not write "Volume".
Until R6 and R7 land, the shipping default must leave the control at 205.
None of 213/225/229 is a product default in this patch.
Baseline: MoKee/android_hardware_qcom_audio
